WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
Let’s talk about what’s happening 
with the war in Libya.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.600
They’ve been fighting over the 
capital Tripoli since last April.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.640
And now, world leaders in Berlin
 have promised to observe

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.640
 an existing arms embargo 
that was being ignored.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.480
But the permanent ceasefire deal 
they had hoped for went nowhere.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.680
After Berlin, people will be watching what this renegade general does next.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.520
But who is Khalifa Haftar?

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.840
 And what’s the battle 
for Tripoli all about?

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.320
The fighting in Libya 
has been on and off 

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.240
ever since the Arab Spring 
uprising in 2011.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.080
Part of the problem was the killing of Muammar Gaddafi that year.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.480
Libya is a country full 
of different tribes

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.120
and Gaddafi’s strategy towards governing Libya for 40 years
 

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.480
was to play those tribes off 
against one another.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.360
Once Gaddafi was out of the picture 
the place became lawless.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.760
Tribes and militias that had fought together to overthrow Gaddafi

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.920
turned against each other to fill the power vacuum created by his death.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.440
Fighting hasn’t really stopped since and if it did it didn’t for long.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.480
And right now there’s a 
battle for Libya’s capital.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.520
The man who wants to take 
over is Khalifa Haftar.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.720
In the late 1960s Haftar was Gaddafi’s friend and helped put him in power.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.200
He became one of Libya’s 
top military leaders.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.240
But in the late ’80s one of Haftar’s missions in Chad went wrong  

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.760
 and long story short he 
fell out with Gaddafi 

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.880
 and ended up living in 
the US for 20 years.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.440
He even became an 
American citizen.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.200
Haftar only came back to Libya 
once the Arab Spring hit.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.240
He eventually set himself up in the east and started consolidating power.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.040
With help from Egypt and 
the United Arab Emirates

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.360
he built what he called the
 Libyan National Army. 

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.560
It’s estimated the LNA has 
at least 25,000 fighters.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.160
Khalifa Haftar's career as we know him today really began in July 2013.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.480
And his premise was quite simple:

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.280
 what Sisi was for Egypt he was 
going to try and be in Libya.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.960
 He realised that there was a need 
for a classical, conventional

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.320
 Arab-Sunni military 
figure with of course

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:33.000
 it goes without saying, an 
autocratic slant to it all.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.520
One thing that’s important
 in understanding Libya  

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.400
is that it has two 
rival administrations.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.600
Haftar and his forces 
back one of them:

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.320
the House of Representatives based 
in the east in the city of Tobruk.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.480
The other is known as the 
Government of National Accord.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.160
The GNA works out of Tripoli 
and is recognised by the UN.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.040
It relies on what’s left of 
Libya’s formal military  

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.400
as well as militias 
to keep control.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.320
But some of Haftar’s allies 
like Egypt and the UAE 

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.160
have a problem with the GNA, 
mainly its links to political Islam
links  

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.880
including the Muslim 
Brotherhood.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.440
Because those ideological 
currents are seen as a threat

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.320
to the regimes that decided 
to support Haftar in 2014.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.360
The problem that Haftar has with any government, whether it's above him 

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.920
 or opposed to him or one that 
has been appointed by him

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.600
 is that he doesn't want 
to share power.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.960
Here’s something else about
 Haftar: he’s unpredictable.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.760
A year ago it looked
 like peace talks 

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.040
were going somewhere and
 the UN and other world

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.520
 powers thought Haftar
 was on board.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.560
But in April 2019, just days before a 
UN peace conference on Libya

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.920
 Haftar surprised everyone with 
an assault on Tripoli.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.920
“Haftar’s forces have been trying
 to seize the capital Tripoli 

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.920
 from the UN-backed 
government.”

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.720
Since then Haftar has been fighting militias loyal to the GNA.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.480
And now he’s got new 
help from mercenaries 

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.280
some of them 
from Russia.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.680
The battle has displaced 
thousands of people

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.400
and more than 200 civilians
 have been killed.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.440
He promised at the time that he was going to be able to, in three days

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.280
 enter Tripoli to eradicate corruption, dismantle all the militias

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.280
and topple the GNA. So it
 was a crazy adventure

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.560
 in the sense that it was 
remarkably ambitious.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:25.240
Right now world powers are trying 
to get the rival sides to agree 

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.200
 to a ceasefire but there are more countries involved in Libya than ever.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:34.320
On the GNA’s side you’ve got the 
UN, Italy, Qatar and Turkey

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.080
 whose parliament recently approved sending ground forces to Tripoli.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.360
But Haftar has important 
friends, too.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.360
Like Egypt, France, Russia, 
Saudi Arabia and the UAE.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:03.800
A lot of states have supported him, mainly the United Arab Emirates 

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:10.480
 who have run a full-blown fleet 
of combat drones out of Libya

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.240
with daily air strikes, 
which have turned out

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.280
  to be very destructive but 
not very effective.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.200
And what are some of the things 
that world powers want?

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.840
Well countries like Turkey want the GNA to survive because among other

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.160
things it wants drilling rights for oil 
and gas in the Mediterranean.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.360
Libya also has a lot of oil 
and countries like Italy 

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.240
have oil companies in there 
that they want to protect.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.160
Others say they’re serious 
about stabilising a country

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:39.960
that’s not had peace
 in far too long.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.560
And if you’re wondering 
how bad it could get

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.480
 Germany says Libya could 
become a “second Syria”.

